Hello All,

I am kicking off the Open Printing Embedded Print (OPEP) solution.   On the
assumption that the PWG will continue to let us use their ftp site, I have
created directory tree (no file content yet) for this activity.  To start
with a clean slated and to avoid confusion with previous work, the new
directory tree is as follows.  (Yes, some content may get duplicated but I
believe starting clean helps.)

ftp://ftp.pwg.org/pub/pwg/ <ftp://ftp.pwg.org/pub/pwg/>  
	- The pwg top level ftp directory

ftp://ftp.pwg.org/pub/pwg/openprinting/
<ftp://ftp.pwg.org/pub/pwg/openprinting/> 
	- (New) The Open Printing top level ftp directory

ftp://ftp.pwg.org/pub/pwg/openprinting/embedded/
<ftp://ftp.pwg.org/pub/pwg/openprinting/embedded/> 
	- (New) The Open Printing Embedded Print top level ftp directory

ftp://ftp.pwg.org/pub/pwg/openprinting/embedded/opepCurrent/
<ftp://ftp.pwg.org/pub/pwg/openprinting/embedded/opepCurrent/> 
	- (New) The OPEP current (some call it latest) directory for all
content
	- (New) There is a more complex directory structure below this one
and
                       I encourage you to review it.

Remember the goal of the project is create an embedded (Linux/POSIX) print
solution; however, the objective is coherence and coherence at all levels.
Therefore, you will see a lot of rigid structure (in directories
conventions, documentation conventions, programing/coding conventions, etc.)
The need for this is based on experience and so that any person can examine
any content and expect a consistent format and structure.   I have found
this type of structuring to reduce confusion while providing high quality
content (document or code).

The next task is to create several evergreen document for;
1. Development activities
2. Tracking activities
3. To do list
4. History 
5. TBD

To start things off, I have created two critial documents; 1.) the
opepHistory and 2.) the opepTermsDefinitions.  The document can be updated
by anyone also long a simple email is sent to the distribution list stating
an update has occurred. 

I had promised to create a development document before the next architecture
meeting.  Well, I started one,  it is located at

ftp://ftp.pwg.org/pub/pwg/openprinting/embedded/opepDevelopment-2007.07.24.p
df
<ftp://ftp.pwg.org/pub/pwg/openprinting/embedded/opepDevelopment-2007.07.24.
pdf>  

I will try to work on it more.



Rgds, 
